The ``train-with, infer-without'' paradigm is not specific to collision avoidance: the same framework could internalize other auxiliary objectives (energy efficiency, human comfort zones) by substituting the SDF head with a corresponding prediction target. Several directions remain open: neural SDF estimation from depth observations for real-world deployment, evaluation on additional VLA backbones (Octo, RT-2), and combining \ours{}'s geometric shaping with constrained RL \citep{zhao2024scpo,zhang2025safevla} for multi-step safety guarantees.

\subsection*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Single benchmark and backbone.} All experiments were conducted on LIBERO/SafeLIBERO with $\pi_{0.5}$. Validating on additional benchmarks (ManiSkill, RoboCasa) and VLA architectures (Octo, RT-2) would strengthen generality claims.

\paragraph{Known geometry assumption.} Ground-truth SDF labels require analytical scene geometry (MuJoCo). Real-world deployment will need neural SDF estimation from depth or point clouds, and the effect of label noise on collision shaping remains uncharacterized.

\paragraph{Simulation only.} All results are simulated; a real-robot validation is needed to confirm that internalized collision awareness survives the sim-to-real gap.

\paragraph{Binary collision definition.} SafeLIBERO defines collision as binary contact (any contact constitutes a collision). For tasks requiring push-through-obstacle motions or compliant contact, the collision penalty may over-suppress necessary interactions---a general limitation of collision-free safety paradigms. Future work could address this via task-conditioned safety margins or force-aware collision thresholds.

